Date: 10 JAN 1980 1058-EST
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
To: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI

First the good news.  The SCHEME chip was powered-up last night
and initial debugging has been started.  One of the packaged chips
has a gross defect in the VDD metalization around the CONTROL PLA,
as noticed by GJS.  The second chip tried has some problem that
shows up whenever it tries to do a nano-sequence.  No clear problem
has been located by optical inspection yet.  

The third chip shows many signs of life however!  It can single-step,
load and read state, and apparently run the garbage collector.  There
are some problems but they seem to be bugs in the interface that
we haven't tracked down yet.

Now the not so good news.  In spite of many simulations, both of the
microcode in LISP on the PDP-10 and also of the circuit, there is a
subtle bug in the GC microcode.  If a node being marked
points to itself in the CAR, the mark-node sequence follows the
CAR again right away.  This is because it tests to see if it is
going to trace in the CAR direction before actually setting the mark
bit for the current node.  The code is below:

(defpc mark-node
       (assign *leader* (&car (fetch *node-pointer*)))
       (cond ((and (&pointer? (fetch *leader*))
		   (not (&in-use? (fetch *leader*))))
	      (&mark-car-being-traced! (fetch *node-pointer*))
	      (&rplaca-and-mark! (fetch *node-pointer*) (fetch *stack-top*))
	      (go-to down-trace))
	     (t 
	      (&mark-in-use! (fetch *node-pointer*))
	      (go-to trace-cdr))))

Date: 9 JAN 1980 0159-EST
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
Subject: History
To: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI


It would be nice to have some record of the errors that were
discovered in the SCHEME chip after it was "finished".  As you might
recall, the chip was finally put together at 6AM PDST on July 19,
1979.  Here is a fragmentary (and probably incorrect) list of the bugs
as I remember them.  I've tried to identify the type of error into the
rough classes of Logic, Artwork (usually a manual error while
ICARUSing), Design-rule, or Program.  I'm also not sure that the
ordering is exactly as the bugs were found.

;;Discoverd by inspection of checkplots
1. Artwork	Micro PLA LSB select wired to wrong point in PLA output drivers.
2. Logic	Timing error in GC-NEEDED pad, which tried to set at the beginning
		  of PHI1 when the conditions tested were not yet valid.
3. Artwork	45 degree swath of Implant on top of the GC-NEEDED pad.
4. Design-rule	Poly-Poly spacing on PH1, Freeze, PH2 contacts.

;;Discovered by hand simulation of layout
5. Design-rule	Pullup/pulldown ratio of ZEROP detector wrong.
6. Logic	Control PLA programming incorrect. PADS-IN looked at READ-STATE
		  instead of LOAD-STATE.
7. Design-rule	(?) Various metal-metal spacings wrong, discovered by Abell.
8. Design-rule	Metal-metal spacing between VDD and GND, bug in PLA synthesis.

;;Discovered during node extraction and simulation
9. Artwork	Extra VDD pad on top of bit-11!
10. Logic	PLA folding was so brain damaged that it was using pass-transistors
		  as logic.
11. Artwork	Extra piece of Poly shorted many signals in NANO PLA wiring.
12. Logic	Control PLA asserted NANO-HOLD even on LOAD-STATE, preventing
		  new state from initializing the NANO PLA.
13. Program	Roundoff error in the CIF generator causes a 1/2 Lambda gap.
14. Program	Microcode compiler code generator for MARK-CAR-BEING-TRACED
		  left off the CDR bit in the memory refernces.
15. Logic	The branch test on POINTER? was inverted.
16. Logic	The TYPE-OF-VAL=BUS branch condition was really testing 
		  ADDRESS-VAL=BUS.
17. Logic	The TYPE-OF-VAL=BUS comparator also compared the MARK bit.

Corrections or additions are solicited.

