Date: 30 November 1979 15:09-EST
From: "Guy L. Steele, Jr." <GLS at MIT-AI>
Subject: YAB
To: BUG-SCHIP at MIT-AI

I agree that probably the mark bit should not be compared at all.

Also, I think that the piece of code UP-TRACE has a bug in it.
The old code is:

(DEFPC UP-TRACE
       (COND ((&=TYPE? (FETCH *STACK-TOP*) GC-SPECIAL-TYPE)
	      (GO-TO SWEEP))
	     (T (ASSIGN *LEADER* (FETCH *STACK-TOP*))
		(COND ((&CAR-BEING-TRACED? (FETCH *LEADER*))
		       (&MARK-CAR-TRACE-OVER! (FETCH *LEADER*))
		       (ASSIGN *STACK-TOP* (&CAR (FETCH *LEADER*)))
		       (&RPLACA-AND-MARK! (FETCH *LEADER*)
					  (FETCH *NODE-POINTER*))
		       (ASSIGN *NODE-POINTER* (FETCH *LEADER*))
		       (GO-TO TRACE-CDR))
		      (T (ASSIGN *STACK-TOP* (&CDR (FETCH *LEADER*)))
			 (&RPLACD (FETCH *LEADER*) (FETCH *NODE-POINTER*))
			 (ASSIGN *NODE-POINTER* (FETCH *LEADER*))
			 (GO-TO UP-TRACE))))))

I believe it should be:

(DEFPC UP-TRACE
       (ASSIGN *LEADER* (FETCH *STACK-TOP*))
       (COND ((&CAR-BEING-TRACED? (FETCH *LEADER*))
	      (&MARK-CAR-TRACE-OVER! (FETCH *LEADER*))
	      (ASSIGN *STACK-TOP* (&CAR (FETCH *LEADER*)))
	      (&RPLACA-AND-MARK! (FETCH *LEADER*)
				 (FETCH *NODE-POINTER*))
	      (ASSIGN *NODE-POINTER* (FETCH *LEADER*))
	      (GO-TO TRACE-CDR))
	     ((&=TYPE? (FETCH *STACK-TOP*) GC-SPECIAL-TYPE)
	      (GO-TO SWEEP))
	     (T (ASSIGN *STACK-TOP* (&CDR (FETCH *LEADER*)))
		(&RPLACD (FETCH *LEADER*) (FETCH *NODE-POINTER*))
		(ASSIGN *NODE-POINTER* (FETCH *LEADER*))
		(GO-TO UP-TRACE))))

That is, one should check car-being-traced *before* checking for
GC-SPECIAL-TYPE.  Otherwise it seems that the CDR of NIL never
gets marked, and (the bug!) the original contents of the CAR of NIL
is never restored.  (I can't understand why the PDP-10 simulation
did not catch this.  Or am I wrong?

Date: 30 NOV 1979 0934-EST
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
Subject: YAB
To: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI

Another bug was turned up by the simulator.  It turns
out that the branch condition on type-val=bus was wrong.
The branch selector input is wired to address-val=bus
instead. I patched this in the simulator database and
have restarted the simulation.  Looking at the chip
it seems easy to swap the two signal wires (that is
probably why the error happened in the first place.)

Alan:
If you look at the top of the register array above the
column that is the EQ comaparator for the VAL register
(about the 7th column of cruft from the left side of the
register array) there are two wires that come out on diffusion
that jumper to metal and run over to an AND gate (producing
val=bus) and one of them goes up to the branch selector.
The easiest thing to do is to swap the two wires where they
jumper to the metal, their order in the AND gate input doesn't 
matter, obviously.

GJS GLS:
While trying to trace down this (the) last bug, it occured to me
that there might be a problem where the end test for the mark
phase which is testing to see if the back pointer coming out
of UP-TRACE is of GC-SPECIAL-TYPE.  This might lose if that
pointer was saved in the CAR of NIL and was marked while there.
The TYPE-VAL=BUS test also compares the mark bit, and it
can be on.  The other places where there are test that might
be screwed by the mark bit, it seems as if that bit can be guarunteed
to be off.  When the machine is running, the mark bits have been
cleared by the final stages of the garbage collector.  In the one
other place in the garbage collector where an EQ test is made,
the only words that can be equal to GC-SPECIAL-TYPE have had their
mark bit cleared when the forwarding pointer was stored.

It seems that one fix might be to flush the output of the mark-bit
cell from the EQ test in the register array.  I don't see
why the mark-bit wants to be compared at all anyway.

The PDP-10 simulator doesn't seem to model the fact that the
mark-bit is really part of the registers, it just assumes
that the registers have just a type field and an address field.

Date: 31 JUL 1979 1207-EDT
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
Subject: Zerop bug
To: ABELL at PARC-MAXC
CC: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI

Probably the most straightforward thing is to edit the
definition of the ZEROP pulldown in Icaraus.  The pullup
could be changed, but overall it is better to beef up
the pulldown because of the capacitive load that the zero
detect has to drive.  The symbol is named ZEROP, and should
have plenty of room to widen the diffusion.  There are
symbols zerop-column, zerop-dummy, etc. but I don't think
that any of those matter.

Date: 28 JUL 1979 1734-EDT
From: GJS at MIT-AI (Gerald Jay Sussman)
Subject: New bugs
To: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI

H and GJS have hand simulated the chip for the first 2 cycles starting
with a load-state of BOOT-LOAD.

Two new bugs have been discovered:
1) The pulldown ratio on the ZEROP detector in the register array is wrong,
	it should be twice as wide.
2) There was a bug in the programming for the contrl pla. The PADS IN term
	was looking at READ-STATE, it should have looked at LOAD-STATE.
	This has been fixed in the source, and there is a new <MIT>CONTRL.CIF.


Date: 20 JUL 1979 1101-EDT
From: H at MIT-AI (Jack Holloway)
To: (BUG SCHIP) at MIT-AI

I've created a mailing list for the Scheme chip.

Bugs to date are:
1)	The LSB of next state coming from the Micro PLA
	spastically comes into the contact of the output
	pullup - not the Poly stub where it should go. (GLS)
2)	The logic in the GC-Needed-Pad was wrong. (H)
	The old logic set a FF on the AND of PH1,
	Set-GC-needed-if-address-newcell=Bus, address-newcell=Bus
	which loses because the newcell=bus condition hasn't had a
	chance to setup when gated by the PH1, resulting in
	the likelyhood that every test GC-needed cycle would
	set the FF.  The new logic has a gate that computes
	(GC-needed and not-Clr-GC-needed) or (Set-GC-needed and
	address-newcell=Bus).  This gate is then held in a
	standard PH1-PH2 latch built onto the pad.  I have
	changed Allpads.IC into new-Allpads.IC with this
	correction.  This will require some wiring changes around
	this pad as well as running PH2 to the pad.  (PH2
	isn't absolutely needed, since the feedback term in
	the input gate will hold the FF state as long as PH1's
	happen often enough.  I just decided to be somewhat
	conservative.)
3)	While editing the Allpads file I noticed a 45 degree
	stripe of implant over the top of the GC-needed-pad,
	in the old version as well as the new.  I've flushed it. (H)
4)	Three is a poly-poly design rule violation at three
	contacts on the rigfht side of the chip - I think
	they are the connections of PH1, Freeze, and PH2
	to the Poly that runs to pads.				(H)

Remember that there is a prize of a free dinner for two at
Louie's for the hacker with the highest count of discovered bugs
(As long as Louie's is still around.)  The decision of the
judges will be final.


